<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indravarma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1st century BCE–1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Indravarman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indravarma was a ruler of the Apracharaja dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajaur">Bajaur</a> in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> around the turn of the era. He is known from coins and from a Buddhist reliquary he dedicated, the Bajaur casket. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspavarma">Aspavarma</a> later served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> and Indo-Parthian overlords.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indravarma.jpg" alt="Indravarma" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apracharajas_Aspavarama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Indravarma, written Iṃdravarma in Kharosthi and called Itravasu on his coins, was the son of the Apracha general Vispavarma. He succeeded the earlier Apracharaja Indravasu and ruled in Gandhara from his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajaur">Bajaur</a>. He may have had a sister named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasavadatta">Vasavadatta</a>, known from the dedication of a water pot, and he is also known from a seal inscription found in Bajaur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Indravarma's two surviving dedications show his family's patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The earlier is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> reliquary, probably dedicated at the end of the 1st century BCE, on which, as Prince Indravarma, son of Commander Vispavarma, he records placing bodily relics in his own stupa together with his wife. The inscription, in Gāndhārī Prakrit and Kharosthi script, honours his father and Sisirena, the commander's wife, and also Indravasu, king of Apraca, and his wife Vasumitra. Because it calls his father commander rather than king, it is thought to be older than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajaur">Bajaur</a> casket.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajaur">Bajaur</a> casket, a reliquary from the Bajaur area, carries a Kharosthi inscription dated to the sixty-third year of the late great king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes</a>, around 5-6 CE. In it Prince Indravarma, son of the king of Apraca, establishes bodily relics of Lord Sakyamuni, brought from the Muraka cave stupa, and shares the religious merit with his mother, Rukhunaka. Between the two inscriptions his father had risen from commander to king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspavarma">Aspavarma</a>, a commander and later a king, is known from an inscription discovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajaur">Bajaur</a> casket shows the involvement of the Apracha kings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in ancient Gandhara, in what is now the north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and of Indravarma in particular.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indravarma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aspavarma, a son of <b>Indravarma</b>, issued coins.</blockquote><a href="/state/apracharajas">Apracharajas</a></li><li><blockquote>The Eastern Gangas emerged in the late 5th century under <b>Indravarman</b> I in Kalinga, on the coast of modern Odisha and northern Andhra, with their early capital at Kalinganagara, modern Mukhalingam.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
